  "worked_solution": "Terminal velocity is the constant speed a falling object approaches as the upward resistive force (drag) grows to exactly balance the downward gravitational force.\nThat is, at terminal velocity (a constant), acceleration is zero, so by $F=ma,$ the resultant force is zero.\n\nFirst, note that only two forces act on the stone: the force due to gravity, given by the weight\n\n$$\nw=mg=15\\cdot 9.8=147\\,\\text{N},\n$$\n\nand the force due to drag $D,$ which is proportional to the square of the velocity (and negative since it acts in the negative direction),\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2}=-2{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the resultant force acting on the stone is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=147-2{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the stone reaches terminal velocity ${v}_{\\text{term}},$ the resultant force is zero:\n\n$$\n147-2({v}_{\\text{term}})^{2}=0\\,\u27f9\\,{v}_{\\text{term}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{147}{2}}=\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the terminal velocity of the stone is ${v}_{\\text{term}}=\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
